Lieberman from itopia.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.400
This session is
really a case study

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.000
or a story of how the
impact of COVID-19

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.490
created a challenge
for TELUS International

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.820
that we were able to quickly
deploy a solution on the itopia

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:37.100
platform to not only take our
customers today into the cloud,

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.650
but to also create a go to
market solution for the Future

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.480
So as mentioned,
I'm Jim Radzicki,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.450
I'm the Chief Technology
Officer for TELUS International,

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.285
and I'd like to introduce
Jonathan Lieberman from itopia.

00:00:51.285 --> 00:00:53.660
JONATHAN LIEBERMAN: It's great
to be with you here today,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.430
Jim, presenting
our success story

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.070
would TELUS International.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.420
We're a Google Cloud technology
partner, premier partner,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.080
that helps enterprises
turn every employee

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.900
into a remote worker
within 24 hours.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.250
JIM RADZICKI: And at
TELUS International,

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.510
we're a global customer
experience digital solutions

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:14.680
provider.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.440
What that means is
we design, build,

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.100
and deliver digital customer
solutions for the world's

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.780
most respected brands.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.390
We've been doing it
for more than 15 years

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:30.220
and those brands include
companies such as Google.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.860
To set the stage a little
bit for the challenge that

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.280
presented us and
particularly around COVID-19,

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.260
I want to share a little
bit about who we are

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.530
and where we operate because
that plays into the story.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.740
So first, TELUS International.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.700
We have nearly 50,000
employees around the globe,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.860
we operate in over 20 countries,
we have more than 600 clients.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:59.270
And we service those clients
in more than 50 languages.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.720
In particular, we also have
a digital solutions arm.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.820
These digital solutions
folks, again, they're

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.000
actually building and designing
digital customer experiences

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.110
for our customers.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.600
We have just over 4,000 folks
who actually do this for us.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.450
We operate those out
of seven countries.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.450
And we have more than 350
clients that are particularly

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.360
focused on digital solutions.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.420
So to set the stage, March 17.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.890
At that moment, of those nearly
50,000 employees that we had,

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.840
TELUS International had none,
actually, of our frontline team

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.350
members who were actually in
a work from home environment.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.330
We had some back office
folks, but really,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.900
from a frontline perspective,
we had zero team members.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.320
If you roll back the clock to
March 17 and think about where

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.000
we were in this
overall pandemic--

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.880
it's amazing how fast
times have changed here--

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.750
but on March 17, one
of the first countries

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.080
to decide to put a stay
at home order in place

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.520
was the Philippines.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.500
And if you look at that
footprint that we had,

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.650
we have a large presence
at TELUS International

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.720
in the Philippines.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.900
Fast forward a little bit in
each of the days coming up--

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.580
actually, in the next 11 days--

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.850
more than six countries
that we operated in

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.910
started to put stay put orders
and stay home orders in place,

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.420
and that trend continued.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.780
So the challenge that
faced us was apparent.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.080
We needed something
to quickly deploy,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.150
that allowed us,
TELUS International,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.540
to take this global workforce
that we had and get them up

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.190
and running in a work
and home environment.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.760
And for that, we
reached out to Google

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.330
to look for a partner who can
help us get to that state.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:47.390
JOHN TARANU: Thanks, Jim.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.720
Hi my name is John Taranu and
I'm the Google Cloud Customer

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.550
Engineer working with
TELUS International

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.160
for the past 3 and 1/2 years.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.100
So TELUS International
reached out to me in a panic,

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.970
just as this whole global
pandemic thing was starting up.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.680
And I'm not a VDI
expert by any stretch,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.520
so I didn't know what
to recommend initially.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.990
So I reached out
to people who are

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.420
VDI experts within
our cloud team,

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.310
and I asked them for
their suggestions,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.360
and they recommended
that I consider itopia.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.890
Now itopia was a Google
Cloud Partner of the Year,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.540
and they had this
fantastic reputation

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.967
of spinning up virtual desktop
solutions rapidly and securely.

00:04:24.967 --> 00:04:27.300
I also reached out to some
of my peer customer engineers

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.520
who had worked
with itopia before,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.110
and they had only
great things to say

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.693
about how responsive
and collaborative

00:04:32.693 --> 00:04:36.700
the itopia team was
with their customers.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.270
So I felt really excited
that we had an opportunity

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.180
to help TELUS International
with this urgent crisis,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.210
and I went out and reached
out to the itopia team.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.530
And that was a sort of a
great collaboration together.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.515
So over to you, Jonathan.

00:04:49.515 --> 00:04:50.890
JONATHAN LIEBERMAN:
Thanks, John.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.860
So I'm sitting at my desk
and up pops this email

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.110
from TELUS International.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.120
This is the actual
email from someone

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.870
with a great sense of
humor asking rhetorically,

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:06.247
if you could launch VDI over the
weekend, that would be great.

00:05:06.247 --> 00:05:07.830
And it turns out
they weren't kidding.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.540
TELUS International
literally needed

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.910
to provide thousands
of workers access

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:15.330
to their mission critical
business applications

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.060
in desktops over
the coming weekend.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.370
Now an email with
this kind of request

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.220
might have struck fear
with other companies,

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.690
but for us, it was just
another day at the office.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.500
We were not at all
fazed by this request

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.840
and our teams were
ready to get to work.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.840
At the most basic level,
itopia turns every employee

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.820
into a remote worker.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.340
We do this through a
solution we call Desktop

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:41.680
as a Service, which
gives employees

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.050
fast and secure access
to their desktops

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:48.050
and apps as though they were
physically in the office.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.480
Now here's Jamie,
who works in one

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.020
of TELUS's international
large delivery centers

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.710
in the Philippines who
is now working from home.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:03.560
Using an e-device, a Chromebook,
MacBook, laptop, PC, or tablet,

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.880
Jamie is able to connect through
a web browser or a small app

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.660
to a full cloud-hosted
desktop or just the apps

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.640
that she uses most.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.000
Instead of the desktop or the
application being installed

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.510
on a server under her desk,
it's being hosted in the nearest

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:24.090
Google Cloud data center,
where the connections are fast,

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:28.200
the security is tight, and the
technology is unparalleled.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.370
So we built itopia to
simplify and modernize

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:34.940
Virtual Desktop
Infrastructure or VDI,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:38.030
as most people commonly
refer to it today.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:42.140
VDI is a technology that's
been around since the mid-'90s.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:45.130
It was invented way before
hyperscale cloud computing

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.950
to help companies enable
their people to work remotely.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:53.960
Unfortunately, VDI got very
complex and costly to install

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:55.860
and maintain.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.620
It consists of multiple stacks
of hardware and software

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.600
from compute to
storage to networking,

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.780
and this requires a lot
of upfront investment

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:06.000
into a bunch of
gear and software

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.270
that sits in the data
center, and begins

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:12.050
to depreciate in value the
minute that you buy it.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.180
And to make matters worse,
it cannot scale on demand

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.270
to handle a situation
like we had with COVID-19,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.750
and it must be managed by
a team of senior engineers,

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:23.820
So what we did at
itopia was completely

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.840
rethink the approach to
VDI by modernizing it

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.570
as a fast to deploy,
simple to use, Cloud

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.170
native solution purpose
built for Google Cloud.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.900
We got rid of the
complex technology stack

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.020
and replaced it with
a unified console that

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.950
can be easily operated by tier 1
tech, who can enable and manage

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:48.360
a worldwide VDI deployment
in any Google data center

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.037
with a single click.

00:07:50.037 --> 00:07:51.120
JIM RADZICKI: Thanks, Jon.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.650
So here we were, a
pandemic facing the world.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.300
Through Google and
the relationship,

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.220
we were introduced
to itopia, who

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.890
showed us and were
impressed with an incredibly

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.180
quick to deploy.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.430
If you think about the times
we're talking about here,

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.800
this sounds like
something you would

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.633
have done over the
course of weeks or months

00:08:08.633 --> 00:08:11.400
with an introduction,
getting the proof of concept.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.440
We were up and running
on the proof of concept

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.380
within the first 24
hours, proving and testing

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.440
the technology to make
sure it could work.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.410
Soon after proving that
it was available to us,

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.450
now we needed to get
into securing it.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.160
So we had to look
at the architecture.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:29.940
We had to make sure that for our
client base, for our employees,

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.470
not only could we
keep them safe,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.049
but we can keep our customers
and their data safe.

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.059
We needed to understand
the architecture

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:38.610
and how that architecture
is flexible enough

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.220
for us to secure the wide
varieties of customers

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:42.280
that we had.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:45.820
So with that, Jon, I flipped it
back over to you to say, hey,

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:46.830
how can we secure it?

00:08:48.887 --> 00:08:50.220
JONATHAN LIEBERMAN: Thanks, Jim.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:53.200
So the novel aspect about
our approach to VDI,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.480
and the reason that we're
able to deploy it so quickly

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.830
is because our solution is pure
automation and orchestration.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.800
There's no software to
install, no hardware

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.340
to buy, procure, and implement.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:07.900
We're automating
what Google already

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:14.000
has in place and everything
runs in our customer's projects.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.100
We consider five key
elements to what we automate

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.410
in our modern DAS deployment.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.030
Number one is the RDS
infrastructure platform itself

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.460
in every single
region that's required

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.650
and in the case of
TELUS International,

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.270
seven regions around the world.

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:34.760
Number two is the
networking which

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:37.760
consist of firewalls,
VPNs, and load balancers--

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.490
again, everything that
Google brings to the table

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.680
from a technology point of view.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:46.190
The third is file servers, which
are used for storage and data

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:47.720
protection.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:51.830
Fourth is the directory services
that leverage Google-managed AD

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:55.640
or allows a customer to spin up
their own domain controllers.

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:57.540
It's a very flexible approach.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:01.040
And then finally, a
management and monitoring

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.440
console, which
leverages Stackdriver

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.570
and Google BigQuery.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:07.670
JIM RADZICKI: Thanks, Jon.

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:10.580
Now we're checking the
boxes for what we had.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:13.910
So March 17, we're
stuck, we needed

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.360
something quick to deploy.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.750
We now have a solution
that we can deploy.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.083
Now we needed to make it secure.

00:10:19.083 --> 00:10:21.500
We needed to make sure that
we can actually keep employees

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.342
safe and customers safe.

00:10:23.342 --> 00:10:25.550
Well, as you saw in the
opening, TELUS International,

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:27.960
we have more than 600 clients.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.310
There is no one solution,
no one setting that's

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:32.930
going to apply to all 600.

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.790
We needed this to
be customizable.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:37.640
We needed it to be refinable.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.140
We needed to know that
we can use it for voice.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.240
We need to know
data-heavy applications.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.260
Some of our clients require
us to use their own VPN.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.660
Some of them didn't want
the desktop to be shown.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:51.090
Some of them only wanted certain
tool sets to be available.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.880
Some of them had
requirements for HIPAA.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.390
Some of them had
requirements-- you name it.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.410
With 600, there were
600 different ways

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:58.600
in which we had this.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.350
For that, I reached
back out to Jon

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:03.650
again to understand how we
can customize and refine

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:04.557
the itopia desktop.

00:11:04.557 --> 00:11:06.140
JONATHAN LIEBERMAN:
That's right, Jim.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.800
So in order to meet TELUS
International's requirements,

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.100
our software enabled the
security policies, rules,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:17.060
permissions from TELUS
International's clients on prem

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.460
ADs to be extended
into Google Cloud.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:24.150
And every situation, as you
can imagine, was different.

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.800
And our solution
acts as a bridge

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:28.560
so that there's no compromise
in security profile

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:32.130
when one of TELUS
International's clients

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:35.520
are being transitioned and
delivered through the cloud.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.560
Now every client
needed to enable

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.040
support for a variety of use
cases from light workloads

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:47.430
to productivity apps to
heavier graphical and media

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:48.900
applications.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.450
And our platform
made it possible

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:54.930
with easy-to-add custom
images to provision workers,

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:58.350
auto scaling of virtual
machines, intelligent user

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.920
routing to the nearest
GCP data center.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.050
And this ensured low latency and
delivered a very unified point

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:09.770
and click console
in every browser.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:13.980
And because our software
is orchestration-only,

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.510
we don't have any access
to the customer's data.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:20.110
This means that from a
regulatory point of view,

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:24.210
our software and the solution
that we deliver through Google

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:26.700
was able to adhere
to the standards

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.580
that TELUS International's
clients demanded,

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:35.770
from SOC to PCI to FINRA,
HIPAA, FedRAMP, you name it.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:36.790
Jim, back over to you.

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:40.410
JIM RADZICKI: Thanks, Jon.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:43.110
So we're checking the boxes
now on all the things,

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:46.528
and again, thinking back, this
was literally days into this.

00:12:46.528 --> 00:12:48.570
It seems, again, like this
would be the things we

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:50.790
do over the course of months.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:52.180
So we were quick to deploy.

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:54.210
We got that proof of
concept up and running.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:57.690
Able to show that we can
get the desktops deployed,

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:59.460
that they were easy to maintain.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:00.450
We had to secure them.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.850
We had to prove to our
clients and our employees

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.280
that we're able to make
it a safe and productive

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.070
environment, keeping
their data as safe

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:10.698
as it would be as if
it were in the office.

00:13:10.698 --> 00:13:11.990
Then we needed to customize it.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:13.365
We had to make it
more refinable.

00:13:13.365 --> 00:13:15.055
Every client has
their requirement

00:13:15.055 --> 00:13:17.430
that needed to be in place,
and we needed to get it done,

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.510
and we check that off, as well.

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:22.920
Lastly, operating around
the globe as we do,

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:24.720
operating with as
many folks as we do,

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.560
we needed to make sure this
solution was globally scalable.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.410
Extremely important in a
virtual desktop environment,

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:32.710
low latency.

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:35.880
The distance between or
the time between where

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.920
the servers are to
where the end points are

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.330
people working from home--

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:40.350
the smaller the better.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.160
It just works better that way.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.490
So for that, we reached
back out to Google

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:49.310
to learn a little bit about how
we can deploy this on the GCP.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.060
JOHN TARANU: So one
of the biggest reasons

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:52.935
the TELUS International
team were considering

00:13:52.935 --> 00:13:56.790
Google Cloud for the solution
is our global footprint,

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:59.220
with over two dozen
regions around the world

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.875
and new regions that are
coming online and regularly.

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:05.000
This is especially important
to TELUS International

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.340
as their employee base is
spread out around the world.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:09.980
The sites in North
and Central America,

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:12.740
many countries in eastern
and western Europe, India,

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:14.870
and the Philippines.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.470
So we designed a
deployment of itopia

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:20.230
for TELUS International in
six Google Cloud regions, such

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.780
that for every TELUS
International geography,

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:24.850
they had at least
two TCP regions

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:28.957
within the same continent
for additional redundancy.

00:14:28.957 --> 00:14:31.290
And the magic that brings
together TELUS International's

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:33.480
employees to their virtual
desktop environments

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:36.170
is one of Google Cloud's
greatest strengths--

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:40.140
a private, secure, high
speed, global backbone.

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.090
That's the same backbone
that already provides

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.220
them access to Google
services that they rely on

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:47.330
in their daily lives.

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:50.060
And this ensures that TELUS
International employees working

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.420
from home around the world have
a connection through their home

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:56.300
internet service provider,
through the existing

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:58.790
in-country high speed peer
connections that Google

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:02.030
has maintained for decades
with telcos around the world,

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:04.370
directly into Google's
backbone, and then

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.190
to their virtual
desktop at Google Cloud.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:09.700
And for each site, there
were redundant network paths

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.180
to take to the servers.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.310
Especially for employees
in places like Guatemala

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:16.620
and the Philippines who
rely on submarine cables

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.530
to connect to the internet,
having a redundant network path

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.140
to a server on the same
continent instead of undersea

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:25.320
cables across the ocean means
that their latency is lower

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:29.180
and their user experience on
their virtual desktop is good.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:30.890
And if TELUS International
tried to build

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:32.750
their own private
network appearing

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:35.750
to serve their work from
home employees like this,

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:37.960
it would be
prohibitively expensive.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:42.650
By relying on Google Cloud,
a global deployment is easy.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:44.510
JIM RADZICKI: So
there's no better scale

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:48.020
than running this on the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:49.700
So now we've checked
all the boxes.

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:53.100
We had it up and running, we
made it serviceable and secure,

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.310
we've refined it for
our customer needs,

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:59.240
we've leveraged the GCP to
scale this around the globe.

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:01.970
And literally, within
just a few days,

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.040
we have stable operations.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.750
So where do we go from here?

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:08.720
Well, as I mentioned
also, TELUS International,

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.190
with our digital
solutions capabilities,

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:14.700
we actually were able to
take this to the market.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:16.550
So in a true sense
of partnership,

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:19.280
not only were we leveraging
this for our own needs,

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:22.940
but we also decided that our
success story is something

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:24.860
that a lot of other
people are going to need,

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:26.600
not only in the
current pandemic,

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:28.920
but as you look into
the future, as well.

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.470
So one more time, I reached
back out to Jon to say,

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:33.920
hey, how can we take
advantage of this in a go

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:36.630
to market strategy.

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:38.550
JONATHAN LIEBERMAN: Thanks, Jim.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.290
The natural next
question is this

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:44.850
since we've gotten it deployed
and successfully onboarded

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:46.680
thousands of
workers, what can we

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.830
do next to make our
partnership even stronger?

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:52.470
And we talked to
your solutions folks

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:55.830
who were so impressed with
the speed and simplicity

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:58.770
of our platform that
we together decided

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:02.310
to integrate it and
bring it to market

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:07.079
as part of your core customer
care suite of solutions.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.750
JIM RADZICKI: Yes, it's
become actually a part

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:14.200
of our overall package now,
what we call Work From Anywhere.

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:16.050
So if you think
about what's required

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.280
for someone to work from home--

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.540
and this is the true
future road of operations

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.190
that we're working toward--

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:24.839
first, you need to connect.

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:27.119
So as you look at TELUS
International's work

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:29.250
from anywhere
solution, the first

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:32.280
is a cloud native
omnichannel-centered platform

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:35.850
that allows folks
to connect with not

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:39.030
only their own companies,
but also with their customers

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:42.720
anywhere and in an internet
connection can reach.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:44.730
Right smack in the
middle of the platform

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.490
is exactly what we just
talked about from itopia--

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.040
to enable that remote
virtual desktop

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.910
so that we can bring not only
the omnichannel solution,

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:56.400
but the full suite of
desktop applications

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:59.610
that were needed with inside
your company or your site--

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:02.950
bring those to the home in
a secure and safe manner.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:05.700
And then lastly, you need
to engage with people.

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:07.980
We talk about all of the
technology through this,

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:09.730
but it's still at
the end of the day,

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:11.520
people who are
working and connecting

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:12.910
with their customers.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:16.200
So a full suite of products for
the customer experience that

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:19.230
allow us to stay meaningfully
engaged with our employees

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.220
while they're working from home.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:23.130
So to give you a
little sense of it,

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.190
here's a snapshot of
actually, those employees

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:29.730
all working from their
at-home environments

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.630
with virtual desktops.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.900
And it wouldn't
